-- allium: 3
-- workspaces.allium
--
-- Scope: Who may see and change what in the shared store. Workspaces, the
--        people in them and their roles; the super-admin; display names;
--        public and private epics and work packages; what every other row
--        about a task inherits from it; how the store enforces all of that
--        on reads as well as writes; and the one-time migration of a store
--        that predates workspaces.
-- Includes: UserProfile, the workspace and membership rules, epic and
--           work-package visibility, the read predicate every table obeys,
--           learning and setting visibility, the migration.
-- Excludes: The Workspace, Membership, WorkspaceRole and Visibility
--           declarations and the visible_to derivations on Epic and Task
--           (core.allium — they are shared model); WorkPackage.visible_to
--           (work-packages.allium); how the board asks for rows
--           (sync.allium); host-scoped write checks (store-split.allium, D11),
--           which stay as they are.
-- Dependencies: UserIdentity, Host, Task, Epic, Learning, Setting, Workspace,
--               Membership from core.allium; WorkPackage from
--               work-packages.allium.
--
-- WHERE THIS STARTS FROM (task #98510). Before this spec the store had no
-- authorisation model. Every table was public: any client that connected
-- could read every row and call almost every reducer. The board chose what
-- to ask for (followed epics plus own creations, sync.allium), but nothing
-- in the store enforced it. The only write checks were D11's host
-- ownership and the scheduled reducers' self-call check.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user (task #98510), recorded so they are not
-- re-litigated:
--   D1. A workspace is where people collaborate. A super-admin creates
--       workspaces, adds people to them and gives anyone any role. A
--       workspace admin adds people to their own workspace and sets roles
--       inside it. Roles are fixed: super-admin (a flag on the person,
--       across all workspaces), and per workspace admin or member. No custom
--       roles.
--   D2. Every epic lives in exactly one workspace; one person may belong to
--       many workspaces. A task inherits its epic's visibility. A task with
--       no epic sits on its owner's user board, which is always private to
--       that owner.
--   D3. A private epic or work package is seen by its creator and nobody
--       else. No admin, workspace or super, reads it. Chosen for privacy
--       over oversight. The cost, accepted: a person who leaves takes their
--       private items out of everyone's sight. The one escape hatch is
--       SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem: a super-admin may DELETE such an item
--       once the person it is private to is no longer a member of its
--       workspace, never read it.
--   D4. Every member of a workspace sees every public epic and work
--       package in it, and who created it, by display name.
--   D5. A sub-epic lives in its parent's workspace. Who a private epic is
--       private to is decided by the NEAREST private epic at or above it
--       (core.allium: Epic.private_holder; resolved with the user, task
--       #98744). So a public sub-epic of a private epic is hidden by that
--       ancestor and seen only by the ancestor's creator, and a private
--       sub-epic of a public epic is its own creator's. Re-parenting may change who an epic or task is private to; that
--       needs the authority a visibility change needs
--       (ReParentingKeepsTheHolder). A root epic moves to another workspace
--       only through MoveEpicToWorkspace (D13), and its whole tree moves
--       with it.
--   D6. A work package links only to items in its own workspace, except a
--       main-board task, which may be linked but stays private to its owner.
--       Seeing a package never reveals a linked item the viewer cannot see.
--   D7. People have a display name. The directory of identities and display
--       names is readable by every connected client, because admins must
--       find people by name to add them.
--   D8. Learnings belong to a workspace: an epic- or task-scoped learning to
--       its item's workspace, a repo-scoped learning to the workspace it was
--       recorded in. A user-scoped learning is private to its author.
--   D9. Settings stay host-scoped (core.allium: Setting), as decided before;
--       a host's settings are visible only to the host's owner.
--   D10. An agent sees what its host's owner sees. Its MCP calls reach the
--       store through its host's board, under that board's identity.
--   D11. The first super-admin is claimed, not configured: claiming works
--       only while the store has none. Accepted risk: in the window between
--       the upgrade and the claim, any connected client could claim first.
--       A super-admin can revoke another, so a wrong claim is recoverable.
--   D12. Migration puts every existing epic and work package into one
--       default workspace as public, and makes every person the store
--       already knows a member of it.
--
-- READ ENFORCEMENT, BY DECISION: VIEWS, NOT ROW FILTERS. SpacetimeDB 2.12
-- ships row-level filters (client_visibility_filter) behind an unstable
-- flag, and its own source says they are not yet enforced. So the store
-- enforces reads by making every table that holds task, epic, package,
-- learning, membership or setting data private and serving clients through
-- per-caller views that apply the visible_to predicates below. The store's
-- publisher identity can still read private tables with one-off SQL. The
-- operator, the trust boundary every sight check exempts, is the store's own
-- identity (the database identity: scheduled calls). The publisher is
-- recognised separately, and only for the seed_* restore reducers
-- (spacetime-seed.allium: actor Operator); it is not exempt from sight checks.
--
-- Proven on a throwaway module (docs/plans/2026-10-28-per-caller-views-spike.md).
-- A client can subscribe to a view with a WHERE; a non-owner's one-off SQL
-- and subscriptions are refused on a private table. Four facts shape the
-- build: (1) a view reads only through indexes, so every column it filters
-- on needs a btree index, and a range read re-runs the view on EVERY write
-- to that table; (2) a view cannot be joined with a private table by the
-- client, so each board ask is its own view; (3) a view's rows have no
-- primary key in the generated bindings, so an update arrives as INSERT(new)
-- then DELETE(old) with no on_update, and the board's apply path must not let
-- the delete erase the updated row; (4) one write re-runs every follower's
-- view, so cost is followers x covered-view size. A view derives a private
-- epic's holder by walking parent_id upward; no private_holder column is
-- stored, so SetEpicVisibility and re-parenting need no subtree cascade.
--
-- IMPLEMENTATION OBLIGATIONS the follow-up tasks inherit. Every new reducer
-- and every new check lives in spacetime/module/src/ and is mirrored in
-- src/sync/memory_caller/ with a pair test in tests/memory_caller_conformance.rs.
-- RULES_VERSION and BOARD_RULES_VERSION move together. New columns are
-- appended last with a default, or the module will not automigrate.

-- THE PER-CALLER VIEWS (task #98690). One view per question the board asks,
-- each answering for ctx.sender() and each built on the module's own
-- visible_to predicates, never a copy of them (the module and the in-memory
-- store run one function over two sets of tables). Rows are the table's own
-- row type, so a card needs no second lookup.
--
--   own_epics, own_tasks, own_packages: what the caller created, plus the
--     epic-less tasks they own, kept only while visible_to still holds (a
--     member who left a workspace loses their own items in it).
--   covered_epics: the epics the caller follows and every descendant, walked
--     by the SERVER at any depth, stopping at an epic the caller cannot see.
--   covered_tasks: the tasks of the covered epics.
--   visible_epics, visible_tasks, visible_packages: everything visible in a
--     workspace the caller belongs to; a client narrows with
--     WHERE workspace_id = N (WHERE epic_id = N for tasks).
--   Rows about a task (agents, dispatch_requests, session_requests,
--     unseen_tasks, task_subagents, learning_retrievals, learning_verdicts):
--     one view each, serving the rows of the tasks the board holds (own
--     tasks and covered tasks). Never wider than the task's own visibility.
--   unseen_tasks is the caller's own marks only.
--   task_watchers is dead (no reducer writes it), so its view is the
--     tasks-held rule above like the rest.
--   poll_owners follow their scope: an epic-scoped row is served while the
--     epic is visible, a task-scoped row while the task is.
--   work_package_links: one row per link, served while the package AND the
--     linked item are both visible to the caller.
--   visible_learnings: the learnings LearningVisibility lets the caller see.
--   own_settings: settings of the hosts the caller owns (D9), and no one
--     else's. Host rows stay readable by all (they carry no task content).
--   visible_workspaces, visible_memberships: the workspaces the caller
--     belongs to and their membership rows (a super-admin sees every one).
--     A view cannot scan a table, so every workspace row carries an indexed
--     listed flag that is always true (appended with a default, task #98691):
--     a super-admin's view reads every row through it.
--
-- Every column a view filters on has a btree index, and every read in a view
-- is a point read, so a write to an unrelated row never re-runs it.
-- The operator (the store's own identity) is not served by views: it reads the
-- tables directly, which is what makes it the trust boundary.
--
-- DISPOSITIONS, agreed with the user (task #98690). todos, task_shells,
-- retired_feed_items and filter_presets become private with no view: no
-- client reads them. handed_out_agent_names stays public (names carry no task
-- content and every board needs the whole set). dispatch_request_timeouts and
-- session_request_timeouts become private. todos and task_shells are dead; a
-- table with rows is refused by a publish that drops it, so dropping them
-- waits for a check that every live store holds none.
--
-- THE PRIVATE FLIP (task #98691). The board subscribes through these views
-- and every table above is private in the same change. usage_events gained an
-- indexed recorded_by column, stamped with the caller by record_usage_event
-- (actor says only human or agent). An event names no task, so the held-task
-- rule cannot apply to it: an event is its recorder's alone. A row recorded
-- before the column existed is nobody's.
--
-- Usage is served ON DEMAND, not through a view (task #98756). A view is
-- recomputed whole when its read set changes, and record_usage_event fires on
-- every keybinding, hook and dispatch, so an own_usage_events view would have
-- re-run over a person's whole history (up to the 100,000-row cap) on every
-- event. The own_usage_summary procedure instead runs when query_usage asks:
-- it reads the caller's rows through the recorded_by index, applies the
-- category, actor and since filters, and returns the grouped counts. The
-- board holds no usage rows and subscribes to nothing for them.

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./work-packages.allium" as wp

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A person as the store presents them to others. One per UserIdentity,
-- created the first time that identity connects (RegisterUserProfile).
entity UserProfile {
    identity: core/UserIdentity
    display_name: String       -- "" until the person sets one; shown as the identity's short prefix meanwhile (config.identity_prefix_length)
    is_super_admin: Boolean = false
    created_at: Timestamp

    invariant OneProfilePerIdentity {
        for a in UserProfiles:
            for b in UserProfiles:
                a != b implies a.identity != b.identity
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    display_name_max_length: Integer = 64
    workspace_name_max_length: Integer = 64
    identity_prefix_length: Integer = 8
    default_workspace_name: String = "Default"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- People and display names --------------------------------

rule RegisterUserProfile {
    when: ClientConnected(identity)

    let existing = UserProfile{identity: identity}

    ensures:
        if not exists existing:
            UserProfile.created(identity: identity, display_name: "",
                                is_super_admin: false, created_at: now)

    @guidance
        -- The module's client_connected lifecycle reducer, beside the
        -- existing rules-version stamp. Idempotent: every reconnect runs it.
}

rule SetDisplayName {
    when: SetDisplayName(name)

    let profile = UserProfile{identity: caller()}
    let trimmed = name.trim()

    requires: exists profile
    requires: trimmed != ""
    requires: trimmed.length <= config.display_name_max_length
    requires: not trimmed.has_control_or_format_characters

    ensures: profile.display_name = trimmed

    @guidance
        -- A person sets only their own name. Names need not be unique: the
        -- directory shows the identity prefix beside a duplicated name.
        -- "Control or format characters" means Unicode categories Cc and Cf
        -- in full (tag characters and Arabic number signs included), plus the
        -- line and paragraph separators U+2028 and U+2029.
}

rule SetOwnNameFromBoard {
    when: SetOwnNameKeyPressed()

    ensures: NameInputOpened()

    @guidance
        -- The TUI twin of SetDisplayName, bound to `u` ("user") in the
        -- board's normal mode and listed in the keybinding table. The input
        -- opens empty. Enter sends
        -- SetDisplayName(typed); an empty or all-blank entry sends nothing
        -- and Esc sends nothing, so the name stays as it was. A refusal from
        -- the store (name too long) shows in the error popup; success shows
        -- "Display name set to <name>" on the status line.
}

rule SetDisplayNameViaMcp {
    when: SetDisplayNameToolCalled(name)

    ensures: SetDisplayName(name)

    @guidance
        -- Agents are first-class users: the MCP twin of SetOwnNameFromBoard.
        -- It sets the name of the identity the agent's host board runs under
        -- (D10). write, idempotent, not destructive. The result names the
        -- saved (trimmed) name. An empty or too-long name is refused with
        -- the store's reason.
}

rule ShownName {
    when: ANameIsShown(identity)

    let profile = UserProfile{identity: identity}

    ensures: shown_name(identity) =
        if exists profile and profile.display_name != "": profile.display_name
        else: identity.prefix(config.identity_prefix_length)

    @guidance
        -- Two twins, because the board cannot link the module crate in a
        -- release build: `shown_name` in the module's user_profile.rs, used
        -- by the module and MemoryReducerCaller, and `models::shown_name`,
        -- used by the board. The conformance scenario asserts they agree;
        -- nothing else re-slices an identity. A creator with no UserProfile row
        -- (a row written before profiles existed) is shown by prefix.
        -- list_work_packages and the package card are today's surfaces that
        -- name a creator: each shows the shown name (CreatorIsShownByName).
}

-- Super-admins ----------------------------------------------

rule ClaimSuperAdmin {
    when: ClaimSuperAdmin()

    let profile = UserProfile{identity: caller()}

    requires: exists profile
    requires: not UserProfiles.any(p => p.is_super_admin)

    ensures: profile.is_super_admin = true
    ensures: AdoptOwnerlessUserBoardTasks(caller())

    @guidance
        -- D11. Works exactly once per store, until the last super-admin is
        -- revoked, which RevokeSuperAdmin forbids. So in practice once.
}

rule AdoptOwnerlessUserBoardTasks {
    when: AdoptOwnerlessUserBoardTasks(super_admin)

    ensures:
        for task in Tasks where task.epic = null and task.owner = null:
            task.owner = super_admin

    @guidance
        -- An epic-less task with no owner predates owners and has nobody to
        -- be private to (D2). The first super-admin takes them; they can
        -- move or delete them from their own user board.
}

rule GrantSuperAdmin {
    when: GrantSuperAdmin(user)

    let granter = UserProfile{identity: caller()}
    let profile = UserProfile{identity: user}

    requires: exists granter and granter.is_super_admin
    requires: exists profile

    ensures: profile.is_super_admin = true
}

rule RevokeSuperAdmin {
    when: RevokeSuperAdmin(user)

    let revoker = UserProfile{identity: caller()}
    let profile = UserProfile{identity: user}

    requires: exists revoker and revoker.is_super_admin
    requires: exists profile and profile.is_super_admin
    requires: UserProfiles.where(p => p.is_super_admin).count > 1

    ensures: profile.is_super_admin = false

    @guidance
        -- A super-admin may revoke themselves, but never the last one: a
        -- store with no super-admin could only be recovered by a fresh
        -- ClaimSuperAdmin race.
}

-- Workspaces ------------------------------------------------

rule CreateWorkspace {
    when: CreateWorkspace(name)

    let creator = UserProfile{identity: caller()}
    let trimmed = name.trim()

    requires: exists creator and creator.is_super_admin
    requires: trimmed != ""
    requires: trimmed.length <= config.workspace_name_max_length
    requires: not Workspaces.any(w => w.name = trimmed)

    ensures: core/Workspace.created(name: trimmed, created_by: caller(), created_at: now)

    @guidance
        -- The creator is not made a member. A super-admin manages a
        -- workspace without belonging to it, and sees its public content
        -- only by adding themselves (AddMember).
}

rule RenameWorkspace {
    when: RenameWorkspace(workspace, name)

    let trimmed = name.trim()

    requires: is_super_admin(caller()) or workspace.is_admin(caller())
    requires: trimmed != ""
    requires: trimmed.length <= config.workspace_name_max_length
    requires: not Workspaces.any(w => w != workspace and w.name = trimmed)

    ensures: workspace.name = trimmed
}

rule DeleteWorkspace {
    when: DeleteWorkspace(workspace)

    requires: is_super_admin(caller())
    requires: workspace.epics.count = 0
    requires: not WorkPackages.any(p => p.workspace = workspace)

    ensures:
        for m in workspace.memberships: not exists m
        for learning in Learnings where learning.workspace = workspace: not exists learning
        not exists workspace

    @guidance
        -- Refused while it holds any epic or package, private ones
        -- included. A super-admin empties it first, using
        -- SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem for private items they cannot read.
        -- That hatch takes only an item whose holder has left the
        -- workspace, so a private item of a person still in it goes by
        -- removing that person first (RemoveMember), or by its holder
        -- deleting it.
        -- A workspace's learnings go with it (they have no other home).
}

-- Membership -------------------------------------------------

rule AddMember {
    when: AddMember(workspace, user, role)

    requires: is_super_admin(caller()) or workspace.is_admin(caller())
    requires: exists UserProfile{identity: user}
    requires: not workspace.has_member(user)

    ensures:
        core/Membership.created(workspace: workspace, user: user, role: role,
                                created_at: now)
}

rule SetMemberRole {
    when: SetMemberRole(membership, role)

    requires: is_super_admin(caller()) or membership.workspace.is_admin(caller())

    ensures: membership.role = role

    @guidance
        -- A workspace may be left with no admin; its super-admins still
        -- manage it. An admin may demote themselves.
}

rule RemoveMember {
    when: RemoveMember(membership)

    let workspace = membership.workspace
    let user = membership.user

    requires: is_super_admin(caller()) or workspace.is_admin(caller())

    ensures:
        not exists membership
        for s in Subscriptions where s.subscriber = user and s.epic.workspace = workspace:
            not exists s
        for u in UnseenTasks where u.subscriber = user and u.task.epic != null and u.task.epic.workspace = workspace:
            not exists u
        -- Task #98746 (V6). A claim must not outlive the sight that made it
        -- workable: every epic-scoped core/PollOwner row on an epic of this
        -- workspace held by a host `user` owns is released, as
        -- UnsubscribeFromEpic does, so another host can claim the feed.
        for owner_row in core/PollOwners.where(p =>
                p.scope = epic and p.scope_id.workspace = workspace
                    and p.host.owner = user):
            not exists owner_row

    @guidance
        -- The person's epics, tasks and packages stay where they are: their
        -- public items stay visible to the remaining members, their private
        -- items are now seen by nobody (D3) until they rejoin or a
        -- super-admin deletes them. A task of theirs that is running keeps
        -- running: removal changes sight, not the host's worktree.
}

-- Epics -------------------------------------------------------

rule EpicIsPrivateOnlyWithACreator {
    when: EpicVisibilityRequested(epic, visibility)

    requires: visibility = public or epic.created_by != null

    ensures: EpicVisibilityAllowed(epic)

    @guidance
        -- A private epic is seen only by its creator, so one with no creator
        -- (created_by null: legacy or feed-made) would be seen by nobody.
        -- Applies to creation (CreateEpicInWorkspace) and to
        -- SetEpicVisibility alike.
}

rule RepoGroupsStayPublic {
    when: EpicVisibilityRequested(epic, visibility)
       or EpicOriginPatched(epic, origin)

    requires: visibility = public or epic.origin != repo_group
    requires: origin = epic.origin
              or (origin != repo_group and epic.origin != repo_group)

    ensures: EpicChangeAllowed(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Task #98744. A repo-group sub-epic (origin = repo_group) is found
        -- by its title and origin by whoever routes a task into the grouped
        -- root (epics.allium: RegroupEpic, route_target), so it must be seen
        -- by everyone who sees its parent. So an epic with origin =
        -- repo_group is never private: set_epic_visibility refuses to make
        -- one private ("a repo-group epic cannot be private"), and
        -- create_epic refuses a private one with that origin
        -- (CreateEpicInWorkspace). A repo-group sub-epic under a private
        -- root is still hidden by that root (D5); it is only never private
        -- itself.
        -- The origin is set by routing alone, so the store's patch_epic
        -- refuses a patch that changes origin to or from repo_group ("a
        -- repo-group origin is set by routing and cannot be changed"). A
        -- patch naming the origin the epic already has is no change and is
        -- let through. No MCP tool takes an origin (core.allium: Epic.origin);
        -- this guards the store against any client that writes the column.
        -- Module: check_repo_group_stays_public, check_origin_patch.
}

rule CreateEpicInWorkspace {
    when: EpicCreated(epic, workspace, parent_epic?, visibility)

    let home = if parent_epic != null: parent_epic.workspace else: workspace

    requires: parent_epic = null or parent_epic.visible_to(caller())
    requires: home.has_member(caller())
    requires: visibility = public or caller() != null
    requires: visibility = public or epic.origin != repo_group    -- RepoGroupsStayPublic

    ensures:
        epic.workspace = home
        epic.visibility = visibility

    @guidance
        -- A root epic whose workspace does not exist is refused, so no path
        -- creates one in workspace 0. Epic 69669 (task #98727) was made on
        -- 2026-10-30 by a build older than the commit that added that check
        -- and kept workspace 0, the column default; MoveEpicToWorkspace is the
        -- rescue for rows like it.
        -- Every epic-creating path (board, create_epic MCP tool, feed
        -- grouping) names a workspace for a root epic. For a sub-epic the
        -- workspace argument is ignored and the parent's is used (D5). A
        -- feed's grouped and role-routed sub-epics are created by the
        -- module under the feed epic and inherit the same way.
}

rule SetEpicVisibility {
    when: SetEpicVisibility(epic, visibility)

    requires: epic.visible_to(caller())
    requires: caller() = epic.created_by
              or epic.workspace.is_admin(caller())
              or is_super_admin(caller())
    requires: visibility = public or epic.created_by != null
    requires: visibility = public or epic.origin != repo_group    -- RepoGroupsStayPublic

    ensures: epic.visibility = visibility

    @guidance
        -- An admin can act only on an epic they can see, one with no
        -- private epic at or above it. Making it private hides it from
        -- everyone but its creator, the admin included, so making a private
        -- epic public again is the creator's call only.
        -- Who may change visibility is narrower than who may write
        -- (resolved with the user, task #98744): changing an epic's
        -- visibility, and any move that changes who an epic or task is
        -- private to (ReParentingKeepsTheHolder, which reuses this
        -- authority), are for the creator, an admin of the epic's workspace
        -- or a super-admin. Every other write on an epic the caller can see
        -- stays open to teammates (WritesRequireSight).
}

rule EpicStaysInItsWorkspace {
    when: EpicMoved(epic, destination)    -- destination null: the epic becomes a root epic

    requires:
        destination = null
        or (destination.workspace = epic.workspace
            and destination.visible_to(caller()))

    ensures: EpicMoveAllowed(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Re-parenting inside one workspace is unchanged; re-parenting across
        -- workspaces is refused. Changing workspace is MoveEpicToWorkspace,
        -- never a side effect of re-parenting. A re-parent that changes who
        -- the epic is private to also needs ReParentingKeepsTheHolder.
}

rule ReParentingKeepsTheHolder {
    when: EpicMoved(epic, destination)          -- destination null: the epic becomes a root epic
       or TaskMoved(task, destination, owner)   -- destination null: onto the user board of `owner`

    -- An epic's holder after the move: its own creator if it is private
    -- itself, else the destination's holder (core.allium: Epic.private_holder).
    let epic_holder_after =
        if epic.visibility = private: epic.created_by
        else if destination != null: destination.private_holder
        else: null

    -- A task has no visibility of its own: in an epic it is the epic's
    -- holder's, and epic-less it is private to its owner (D2).
    let task_holder_before =
        if task.epic != null: task.epic.private_holder else: task.owner
    let task_holder_after =
        if destination != null: destination.private_holder else: owner
    let task_workspace =
        if destination != null: destination.workspace
        else if task.epic != null: task.epic.workspace
        else: null

    requires:
        epic_holder_after = epic.private_holder
        or caller() = epic.created_by
        or epic.workspace.is_admin(caller())
        or is_super_admin(caller())
    requires:
        task_holder_after = task_holder_before
        or caller() = task_holder_before
        or (task_workspace != null and task_workspace.is_admin(caller()))
        or is_super_admin(caller())

    ensures: MoveAllowed()

    @guidance
        -- Task #98744, resolved with the user. A move that changes who an
        -- item is private to (into a private epic, out of one, or between
        -- two) is a visibility change, so it needs the authority
        -- SetEpicVisibility needs, on top of sight (EpicStaysInItsWorkspace,
        -- TaskEntersAnEpicTheCallerCanSee). A move that keeps the holder
        -- needs nothing extra.
        -- For an epic the authority is over the epic MOVED, never the
        -- destination: its creator, an admin of its workspace, or a
        -- super-admin. It covers a move to the root as well. A private epic
        -- is its own creator's wherever it sits, so only a public epic's
        -- move can change its holder; that move takes its public subtree
        -- with it. Refusal: "only the creator or an admin may move an epic
        -- into or out of a private epic". Module: check_epic_move,
        -- epic_move_changes_holder (shared with MemoryReducerCaller).
        -- A task has no creator, so the authority is the person it is
        -- private to now (their own work is theirs to expose or keep), an
        -- admin of the workspace it moves into (or out of, for a move onto
        -- a user board), or a super-admin. The destination is the epic the
        -- task lands in after repo-group routing (route_target), which
        -- shares its grouped root's holder. A task detached onto a user
        -- board lands on the CALLER's board (identity stamping, below), so
        -- the board is the caller's: moving a task the caller sees off a
        -- public epic onto it is allowed for any member (task #98783). It
        -- hides nothing the caller could not already change. Moving a task
        -- off someone else's private epic onto it still needs the authority
        -- above. Refusal: "task N: only the person it is private to, or an
        -- admin, may move it into or out of a private epic". Module:
        -- check_task_move, task_move_permitted (shared with
        -- MemoryReducerCaller).
        -- The store itself (the operator) is exempt, as from every sight
        -- check (WritesRequireSight). Separately, the repo-group relinks
        -- (RegroupEpic, FlattenEpic, the reroute after a repo_path change)
        -- need no check from anyone: a repo-group sub-epic is always public
        -- (RepoGroupsStayPublic), so it shares its grouped root's holder and
        -- a task moving between them keeps its holder.
}

rule MoveEpicToWorkspace {
    when: MoveEpicToWorkspace(epic, destination)

    let source = epic.workspace    -- may name a workspace that does not exist

    requires: epic.parent = null
    requires: exists destination
    requires: destination != source
    requires: epic.private_holder = null or epic.private_holder = caller()
    requires: epic.subtree.all(n => n.private_holder = null or n.private_holder = caller())
    requires:
        is_super_admin(caller())
        or (exists source and source.is_admin(caller())
            and destination.is_admin(caller()))

    ensures:
        for node in epic.subtree:
            node.workspace = destination
        for learning in Learnings
                where learning.scope in {epic, task}
                  and learning.item_in(epic.subtree):
            learning.workspace = destination
        for link in WorkPackageLinks
                where link.item in epic.subtree or link.item in epic.subtree.tasks:
            not exists link
        for follow in Subscriptions where follow.epic in epic.subtree:
            if not destination.has_member(follow.subscriber):
                not exists follow
        -- Task #98746 (V6). A host whose owner is not a member of the
        -- destination loses sight of the tree, so its epic-scoped
        -- core/PollOwner claims on the tree are released: the feed pauses
        -- until a host that can see the epic claims it, never stalls.
        for owner_row in core/PollOwners.where(p =>
                p.scope = epic and p.scope_id in epic.subtree
                    and not destination.has_member(p.host.owner)):
            not exists owner_row

    @guidance
        -- A refusal for a missing, unseen or private-to-someone-else epic
        -- reads "epic N does not exist": it never says which. A super-admin
        -- needs no membership in the source, and the epic need not be
        -- visible to them: workspace 0 has no members, so nobody could see
        -- it, and the move is the rescue. Admin of both is checked against
        -- real memberships, so a source that does not exist fails it and
        -- falls to the super-admin branch with no special case for 0.
        -- A refusal for a sub-epic names its root so the caller can retry.
        -- The same workspace is refused rather than treated as a no-op,
        -- so a caller never thinks it moved something.
        -- Unseen marks of people who lose sight of the epic are dropped
        -- with their follows, as RemoveMember does for a whole workspace.
}

-- Tasks -----------------------------------------------------

rule TaskEntersAnEpicTheCallerCanSee {
    when: TaskPlacedInEpic(task, epic)

    requires: epic.visible_to(caller())

    ensures: TaskPlacementAllowed(task, epic)

    @guidance
        -- Covers task creation in an epic and moving a task into one. An
        -- epic-less task needs no sight check: it lands on the caller's own
        -- user board (core.allium: OwnerTracksUserBoardTask). A move, onto a
        -- user board included, that changes who the task is private to also
        -- needs ReParentingKeepsTheHolder.
}

rule TaskMovedToAnotherWorkspace {
    when: TaskMoved(task, destination, owner)    -- destination null: onto the user board of `owner`

    let before = if task.epic != null: task.epic.workspace else: null
    let after = if destination != null: destination.workspace else: null

    requires: before != null and after != null and before != after

    ensures:
        for link in WorkPackageLinks where link.item = task:
            not exists link
        for learning in Learnings
                where learning.scope = task and learning.item_in(task):
            learning.workspace = after

    @guidance
        -- Task #98744. The task twin of MoveEpicToWorkspace's clean-up, and
        -- what keeps LinkedItemsShareThePackagesWorkspace true: the task's
        -- package links were the old workspace's, so they go, and its
        -- task-scoped learnings follow it into the new workspace (D8). The
        -- task and the packages are otherwise untouched. A move inside one
        -- workspace keeps both.
        -- Only a move between two workspaces cleans up: a task on a user
        -- board has no workspace and may be linked to a package anywhere
        -- (D6), so a move onto or off a board keeps its links.
        -- Module: move_task_row (set_task_epic, update_task).
}

-- Work packages ----------------------------------------------

rule CreateWorkPackageInWorkspace {
    when: WorkPackageCreated(package, workspace, visibility)

    requires: workspace.has_member(caller())

    ensures:
        package.workspace = workspace
        package.visibility = visibility
}

rule SetWorkPackageVisibility {
    when: SetWorkPackageVisibility(package, visibility)

    requires: package.visible_to(caller())
    requires: caller() = package.created_by
              or package.workspace.is_admin(caller())
              or is_super_admin(caller())

    ensures: package.visibility = visibility
}

rule LinkOnlyInsideTheWorkspace {
    when: LinkRequested(package, item)

    requires: package.visible_to(caller())
    requires: item.visible_to(caller())
    requires: item_workspace(item) = null or item_workspace(item) = package.workspace

    ensures: LinkItem(package, item)

    @guidance
        -- item_workspace is the epic's workspace for an epic, the epic's
        -- workspace for an epic task, and null for a main-board task, which
        -- may be linked anywhere and stays private to its owner (D6).
}

-- Naming a workspace and a visibility, from the board and over MCP ---------
--
-- Task #98519. Agents are first-class users: each board rule has an MCP twin
-- (docs/mcp-tool-design.md).

rule NameWorkspaceAndVisibilityViaMcp {
    when: McpCreateEpic(title, workspace_id?, visibility?, parent_epic_id?)
       or McpCreateWorkPackage(title, workspace_id, visibility?)
       or McpUpdateEpic(epic, visibility)
       or McpUpdateEpic(epic, workspace_id)
       or McpUpdateWorkPackage(package, visibility)

    ensures:
        EpicCreated(epic, workspace_id, parent_epic_id, visibility ?? public)
        WorkPackageCreated(package, workspace_id, visibility ?? public)
        SetEpicVisibility(epic, visibility)
        SetWorkPackageVisibility(package, visibility)
        MoveEpicToWorkspace(epic, workspace_id)

    @guidance
        -- create_epic: workspace_id is required for a root epic and left
        -- out for a sub-epic (parent_epic_id given), which takes the
        -- parent's; naming one with a parent is refused, so a caller never
        -- thinks it chose a workspace it did not. create_work_package:
        -- workspace_id is always required. There is no default workspace and
        -- no inheritance from the agent's task (a required parameter, with the
        -- person's workspaces and their ids named in the tool description and
        -- in the refusal for a missing or unknown one).
        -- visibility is "public" or "private", default public. update_epic
        -- takes visibility and workspace_id (a move, MoveEpicToWorkspace);
        -- update_work_package takes visibility only. Every refusal is the
        -- store's.
        -- Results name the workspace and the visibility; list_epics and
        -- list_work_packages show both. list_work_packages also shows the
        -- creator by display name (CreatorIsShownByName); an epic row does
        -- not carry its creator on the board yet.
}

rule NameWorkspaceAndVisibilityFromBoard {
    when: EpicOrPackageFormOpened(kind, parent?)

    let mine = Memberships.where(m => m.user = caller())

    ensures:
        if parent = null:
            WorkspaceChosen(form, if mine.count = 1: mine.first.workspace else: ChoiceRequired)
        VisibilityChosen(form, public)

    @guidance
        -- The new-epic and new-package forms (and the editors that create
        -- from them) end with a Workspace field and a Visibility field.
        -- Workspace shows only when the person belongs to more than one
        -- workspace, preselected to the first; with exactly one it is used
        -- without asking. A person in none cannot create an epic or package,
        -- and sees "You belong to no workspace; ask a super-admin to add you".
        -- A sub-epic form has no Workspace field (the parent's is used).
        -- The step is one screen after the title (and, for an epic, the
        -- description): j/k move between workspaces, Tab, BackTab or h/l
        -- toggle visibility (default public; private shows a lock marker),
        -- Space or Enter creates, as Space/Enter choose in every other
        -- picker (task #98822), and Esc leaves nothing created (a package
        -- returns to its picker). The workspaces are read when the form opens; a person in
        -- none is refused there, not at the end.
        -- Editing an epic (`e`, the pop-out document) shows a VISIBILITY
        -- section, changeable, and a WORKSPACE section that is read-only and
        -- ignored on save (moving an epic is MCP-only for now, see the open
        -- question below). A package's visibility switches with `V` on its
        -- card, which shows "private" when it is. Who may change either is
        -- the store's call (SetEpicVisibility / SetWorkPackageVisibility);
        -- its refusal shows in the error popup.
}

-- The escape hatch -------------------------------------------

rule SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem {
    when: SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem(item)

    requires: is_super_admin(caller())
    requires: item_is_private(item)
    requires: item_holder(item) = null
              or not item_workspace(item).has_member(item_holder(item))

    ensures: ItemDeleted(item)

    @guidance
        -- D3. item is a private epic (with its subtree, as any epic delete)
        -- or a private work package. item_is_private holds for an epic that
        -- is effectively private, private itself or under a private ancestor
        -- (core.allium: Epic.is_effectively_private; its holder is the
        -- nearest private epic's creator, D5), and for a package whose own
        -- visibility is private. item_holder is
        -- the epic's private_holder or the package's created_by; null when
        -- the row names nobody (a legacy row). item_workspace is the epic's
        -- or the package's own workspace.
        -- The hatch is for items NOBODY can see (task #98744): the holder
        -- must no longer be a member of the item's workspace, or there must
        -- be no holder at all. A private item whose holder is still a member
        -- is theirs to delete, and is refused here ("only an item nobody can
        -- see is deleted this way"). A public item is refused too, so the
        -- hatch is no way around DeleteEpic for ordinary items. Module:
        -- epic_is_unseen, package_is_unseen. UnseenItemListing shows the
        -- same items, an epic tree by its topmost private epic
        -- (admin-surfaces.allium: UnseenItemListingContents). The
        -- super-admin chooses from that listing, which shows no titles or
        -- contents, and the delete goes through the normal delete path: an
        -- epic still needs every task in its subtree done (DeleteEpic's
        -- guard), so the hatch never tears down running work.
}

-- Writes ------------------------------------------------------

rule WritesRequireSight {
    when: WriteToItem(item)

    requires: item.visible_to(caller())

    ensures: WriteAllowed(item)

    @guidance
        -- Every reducer that changes, dispatches, wraps up, closes, links,
        -- subscribes to or rates a task, epic, package or learning first
        -- checks the caller can see it. A refusal names no detail of the
        -- item, so a refusal cannot be used to probe for a private one.
        -- Teammates may still act on each other's work in a workspace they
        -- share (learning #90175); this narrows that to work they can see.
        -- Two writes need more than sight, because they decide who sees an
        -- item: a visibility change (SetEpicVisibility,
        -- SetWorkPackageVisibility) and a move that changes who an item is
        -- private to (ReParentingKeepsTheHolder). Those are for the creator
        -- (for a task, the person it is private to), an admin or a
        -- super-admin.
        -- Unlinking an item the caller cannot see from a package is a
        -- no-op, as for an item that is not linked (work-packages.allium:
        -- UnlinkItem).
        --
        -- UnseenIsAsMissing (task #98519). An item the caller cannot see is
        -- answered exactly as an item that does not exist: a reducer that
        -- ignores a missing id ignores an unseen one, and one that refuses a
        -- missing id refuses an unseen one with the same words. So the
        -- answer never tells a caller whether a private item is there.
        -- One exception is named: deleting an epic cascades through its
        -- subtree, so it is refused ("cannot delete right now") when the
        -- subtree holds an epic the caller cannot see, rather than taking
        -- someone's private sub-epic with it.
        --
        -- The operator (the store's own scheduled calls) is exempt from every
        -- sight check: it is the trust boundary (see READ ENFORCEMENT), not a
        -- person with a membership. The publisher is not exempt; it only may
        -- call the seed_* reducers.
        --
        -- Identities are the caller's, never a client's claim. A created epic
        -- or work package is stamped created_by = the caller whatever the row
        -- says; subscribe_to_epic, unsubscribe_from_epic and mark_task_seen
        -- refuse a subscriber that is not the caller. Only the operator may
        -- name another identity.
        --
        -- The same holds for the owner of a user-board task (task #98755, V4).
        -- A task created with no epic, or detached from its epic (set_task_epic,
        -- update_task), lands on the CALLER's own board: owner = the caller
        -- whatever the argument says. Without that, Bob could detach a
        -- teammate's task with owner = Bob and take it, or with an owner no one
        -- is and hide it. A created task (create_task, create_task_signalled)
        -- is also stamped created_by = the caller. The `owner` argument stays
        -- in the signatures for the operator, the one caller who may name
        -- another person. A task that joins an epic carries no owner, as
        -- before.
        --
        -- Module-made epics. A feed's grouped and role-routed sub-epics
        -- inherit the parent's workspace. A managed feed's root epics
        -- (ensure_managed_epics) are created in the calling person's
        -- lowest-numbered workspace, and not at all for a person in none.
        --
        -- Hooks and ingest. The agent hooks (stop, pre-tool, notification,
        -- prompt, subagent), PR-learnings gate and feed ingest are writes like
        -- any other: an unseen task or epic is answered as a missing one. A
        -- managed root epic is found only among epics the caller can see, so a
        -- second person in another workspace gets a root of their own.
        -- Module: the hook reducers (try_record_stop, record_pre_tool_use,
        -- record_notification, record_user_prompt_submit,
        -- mark_pr_learnings_gate_shown, subagent_*) read the task through
        -- visible_task; ingest_feed reads its epic through visible_epic;
        -- ensure_managed_epics looks only among visible epics. Host-internal
        -- calls are not exempt: only the operator is.
        --
        -- Created-by. A new epic with no creator is stamped with the caller,
        -- so a private epic always has someone to be private to.
}

-- Learnings and settings -------------------------------------

rule LearningVisibility {
    when: LearningRecorded(learning, recorder)

    ensures:
        learning.author = recorder
        if learning.scope = user:
            learning.workspace = null
        else if learning.scope = epic or learning.scope = task:
            learning.workspace = scope_item(learning).epic_workspace
        else:
            learning.workspace = recording_workspace(recorder)

    @guidance
        -- D8. The store stamps author (the recorder's identity) and workspace
        -- itself; a client cannot choose either. A learning with a workspace
        -- is seen by that workspace's members, and an epic- or task-scoped
        -- one also only while its item is visible to the reader. A learning
        -- with no workspace is seen only by its author: that is every
        -- user-scoped learning, and every other learning whose item or
        -- recorder has no workspace.
        -- recording_workspace is the workspace of the recording agent's
        -- task's epic. A repo-scoped learning recorded from a main-board
        -- task, or by a person with no task, has none, so it is private to
        -- its author like the user board it came from (resolved with the
        -- user, task #98523; there is no personal workspace, D2). The
        -- same holds for a task-scoped learning on a main-board task.
        -- The workspace follows the item afterwards: an epic tree moved
        -- (MoveEpicToWorkspace), a task moved (TaskMovedToAnotherWorkspace)
        -- and an epic-scoped learning re-scoped to another epic
        -- (rescope_epic_learnings; learnings.allium:
        -- ReScopeLearningsOnRepoGroupDelete) all take the new item's
        -- workspace.
        -- A learning that predates workspaces has no author. One with a
        -- workspace keeps that workspace's members; a user-scoped one stays
        -- visible to everyone, as it was, since nobody can claim it.
        -- query_learnings, prompt injection and get_learning all apply the
        -- same reader predicate, so an agent is never shown, and never
        -- records a retrieval of, a learning its host's owner cannot see.
}

-- Migration --------------------------------------------------

rule MigrateStoreToWorkspaces {
    when: StoreUpgradedToWorkspaces()

    requires: Workspaces.count = 0

    ensures:
        let default = core/Workspace.created(name: config.default_workspace_name,
                                             created_by: store_publisher(),
                                             created_at: now)
        for epic in Epics:
            epic.workspace = default
            epic.visibility = public
        for package in WorkPackages:
            package.workspace = default
            package.visibility = public
        for learning in Learnings where learning.scope != user:
            learning.workspace = default
        for person in known_identities():
            if not exists UserProfile where identity = person:
                UserProfile.created(identity: person, display_name: "",
                                    is_super_admin: false, created_at: now)
            core/Membership.created(workspace: default, user: person,
                                    role: member, created_at: now)

    @guidance
        -- D12. Runs once, on the first connection after the module that
        -- adds workspaces is published, beside backfill_all_agents. "Once"
        -- means once per store, not once per rules version: the module runs it
        -- only when the store's stamped rules version is older than the one
        -- that added workspaces (rules.rs: WORKSPACES_RULES_VERSION), so a
        -- later bump never brings back a default workspace a super-admin
        -- deleted. A brand-new store counts as older and gets an empty
        -- default workspace, which is where its first people are added.
        -- store_publisher is the store's own identity (the database
        -- identity, in the spelling of caller()), not a person: the migration
        -- is not any client's act.
        -- known_identities is every distinct non-empty Host.owner,
        -- Task.owner, Task.created_by, Epic.created_by,
        -- WorkPackage.created_by and Subscription.subscriber. A person who
        -- already has a profile keeps it, name included. Nobody is an
        -- admin yet: the super-admin claims first (ClaimSuperAdmin), then
        -- promotes. Existing epic-less tasks keep their owner and become
        -- private to them; ownerless ones wait for the first super-admin
        -- (AdoptOwnerlessUserBoardTasks).
        --
        -- Snapshots: user_profiles, workspaces and memberships are shared
        -- tables (spacetime-seed.allium: SharedTable), so a restore keeps
        -- every workspace id the epics point at.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Should the TUI epic editor's WORKSPACE section become editable, listing the workspaces the person administers, so a move is also a board action? MoveEpicToWorkspace ships over MCP only (task #98727); the board path waits for a decision."

invariant SubEpicSharesItsParentsWorkspace {
    for epic in Epics:
        epic.parent != null implies epic.workspace = epic.parent.workspace
}

invariant LinkedItemsShareThePackagesWorkspace {
    for link in WorkPackageLinks:
        item_workspace(link.item) = null
            or item_workspace(link.item) = link.work_package.workspace
}

invariant AtLeastOneSuperAdminOnceClaimed {
    UserProfiles.any(p => p.is_super_admin)
        or not ever_claimed_super_admin()
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Person {
    identified_by: UserProfile where identity != null
}

actor SuperAdmin {
    identified_by: UserProfile where is_super_admin = true
}

actor WorkspaceAdmin {
    within: core/Workspace
    identified_by: UserProfile where within.is_admin(identity)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a connected client receives from the store. One surface for all
-- tables: the predicate is the same everywhere, only the item changes.
surface StoreReads {
    facing reader: Person

    exposes:
        for profile in UserProfiles:
            profile.identity
            profile.display_name
            profile.is_super_admin
        for w in Workspaces where w.has_member(reader.identity) or reader.is_super_admin:
            w.name
            w.memberships
        for epic in Epics where epic.visible_to(reader.identity):
            epic
            epic.created_by
        for task in Tasks where task.visible_to(reader.identity):
            task
            task.created_by
        for package in WorkPackages where package.visible_to(reader.identity):
            package
            package.created_by
            package.links where link_item_visible(link, reader.identity)
        for setting in Settings where setting.host.owner = reader.identity:
            setting

    provides:
        SetDisplayName(name)
        SetEpicVisibility(epic, visibility)
        SetWorkPackageVisibility(package, visibility)
        ClaimSuperAdmin() when not UserProfiles.any(p => p.is_super_admin)

    @guarantee AClientReceivesOnlyWhatItCanSee
        -- A client never receives a row it cannot see: not by subscription,
        -- not by one-off SQL, not by a reducer's error text. This holds for
        -- every row ABOUT a task too: agents, dispatch and session
        -- requests, unseen marks, watchers, subagent rows, PR poll state and
        -- learning retrievals and verdicts are visible only when the task
        -- they name is (the views serve them for the tasks the board holds). Poll-owner rows follow their scope (an epic or a
        -- task). A usage event names no task, so it is its recorder's alone
        -- (own_usage_summary). Host rows carry no task content and stay
        -- readable by all.
        --
        -- Follows are private too: the subscriptions table is served only
        -- through own_subscriptions (the caller's own follows of epics they
        -- can still see), so one-off SQL never reads another person's
        -- follow or a private epic's id. Proven by the live test
        -- a_non_member_cannot_see_who_follows_a_private_epic
        -- (tests/spacetime_module/read_enforcement.rs). The other public
        -- tables (hosts, user_profiles, repo_paths, repo_base_branches,
        -- handed_out_agent_names, schema_version, rules_version) carry no
        -- item content.

    @guarantee CreatorIsShownByName
        -- D4. Wherever a card or a tool result names who created an item,
        -- it shows the creator's display name, or the identity prefix when
        -- they have set none.

    @guidance
        -- Implemented as private tables plus per-caller views (see the
        -- header). The board's own_creations and covered_epics asks
        -- (sync.allium) are then answered from the views and can never
        -- widen past them.
}

surface WorkspaceAdministration {
    facing admin: WorkspaceAdmin
    context workspace: core/Workspace

    exposes:
        workspace.name
        workspace.memberships

    provides:
        AddMember(workspace, user, role)
        SetMemberRole(membership, role)
        RemoveMember(membership)
        RenameWorkspace(workspace, name)

    @guidance
        -- Both a TUI surface and MCP tools (agents are first-class MCP
        -- users): admin-surfaces.allium (task #98522) specs the screen, the
        -- tools and how a person is found by display name.
}

surface SuperAdministration {
    facing admin: SuperAdmin

    exposes:
        Workspaces
        UnseenItemListing

    provides:
        CreateWorkspace(name)
        RenameWorkspace(workspace, name)
        DeleteWorkspace(workspace)
        AddMember(workspace, user, role)
        SetMemberRole(membership, role)
        RemoveMember(membership)
        GrantSuperAdmin(user)
        RevokeSuperAdmin(user)
        SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem(item)

    @guarantee UnseenItemListingShowsNoContent
        -- Served by the module's `unseen_items` view (admin-surfaces.allium:
        -- UnseenItemListingContents), which is empty for anyone but a
        -- super-admin; task #98690 folds it into the per-caller views. The
        -- delete keeps DeleteEpic's every-task-done guard (the code calls
        -- validate_epic_deletable), so the hatch never tears down running work.
        -- The listing of private items whose holder (an epic's
        -- private_holder, a package's creator) is no longer a member of
        -- their workspace, or who have none, shows kind, id, workspace,
        -- creator and created date. Never a title, description or any child
        -- row. These are the items SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem accepts.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "TaskMovedToAnotherWorkspace: the store treats a user board as a workspace of its own (none), so a task moved onto or off a user board drops all its package links even when the package is in the epic's workspace, and D6 lets a main-board task be linked to a package anywhere. Should a move onto a user board keep the links (D6), and a move off one keep those in the destination epic's workspace? The approved rule (task #98744) names only a move to an epic in another workspace."

open question "ReParentingKeepsTheHolder makes moving a task off a public epic onto a member's own user board a holder change (nobody to that member), so only an admin or super-admin may do it. Is that intended, or should a member be able to pull a visible public task onto their own board?"

open question "UnseenItemListingContents lists only private epics with no private ancestor (A5), but under the nearest-holder reading (D5) a private sub-epic of a private epic is its own creator's. When that sub-epic's creator leaves while the ancestor's creator stays, SuperAdminDeletesUnseenItem accepts it but the listing never shows it. Should the listing show every private epic whose own holder has left?"

open question "SetWorkPackageVisibility has no counterpart to EpicIsPrivateOnlyWithACreator, and the store does not stamp created_by on a new package. Should a package with no creator be refused as private?"

open question "'PR poll state' has no table of its own: the PR state lives on the task and poll_owners (scope task) follows its task. Is anything else meant?"
